Show the workspace's own processes in the web Resource Manager

Resource Manager showed all zeros in the tile: the web preload's
`memory.getSnapshot` was an empty-snapshot stub.

It now routes to the runtime's `diagnostics.memory` method, which paired phones
already poll, so the tile reports the workspace host's processes, RAM and CPU.
The empty snapshot is the answer for one condition only - no environment
connected - tested for via `requireActiveEnvironmentOrNull()` the way every
sibling stub in this file does, rather than inferred from a failed call. A
connected runtime that cannot answer must reject: the store sets
`memorySnapshotError` from a rejection alone and `ResourceUsageStatusSegment`
derives `daemonUnreachable` from it, so swallowing the failure would make the
"daemon not responding / Restart" banner unreachable in the tile.

To test, open Resource Manager in the tile and confirm it lists the workspace's
processes rather than zeros; then revoke the device and confirm the pane says
the daemon is not responding. Covered by `web-resource-manager.test.ts`, which
separates the two failure shapes - a transport rejection and an `ok:false`
envelope - from the unpaired case.
Index: b/lib/orca/src/renderer/src/web/web-preload-api.ts
===================================================================
--- a/lib/orca/src/renderer/src/web/web-preload-api.ts
+++ b/lib/orca/src/renderer/src/web/web-preload-api.ts
@@ -905,7 +905,19 @@ function createWebPreloadApi(): Partial<
         }))
     },
     memory: {
-      getSnapshot: () => Promise.resolve(createEmptyMemorySnapshot())
+      // Why: the Resource Manager should show the RUNTIME's processes — its
+      // terminals and agents run on the server, not in this browser. The
+      // server's diagnostics.memory returns the same MemorySnapshot shape the
+      // desktop main builds locally (phones already poll it); fall back to the
+      // empty snapshot only when no environment is connected.
+      getSnapshot: () => {
+        if (!requireActiveEnvironmentOrNull()) {
+          return Promise.resolve(createEmptyMemorySnapshot())
+        }
+        // Why: a connected runtime that fails to answer must reject, or the pane's
+        // "daemon not responding" banner can never appear — it derives from this rejection.
+        return callRuntimeResult<MemorySnapshot>('diagnostics.memory')
+      }
     },
     aiVault: createAiVaultApi(),
     preflight: createPreflightApi(),
